<div class="legal-deep-dive" style="margin-top: 30px; line-height: 1.8;">
    <h2 style="color: var(--primary);">Comprehensive Guide to Family Partition Deeds & Property Division in India</h2>
    <p>A <strong>Partition Deed</strong> is a critical legal instrument under the <strong>Indian Succession Act, 1925</strong> and Hindu Succession Act, 1956 used to divide joint family property among co-owners or legal heirs. Unlike a Release Deed or Gift Deed, a Partition Deed establishes independent, exclusive ownership rights over specific portions or shares of a ancestral or jointly acquired estate.</p>

    <h3>Statutory Requirements for Execution & SRO Registration</h3>
    <ul style="padding-left: 20px;">
        <li><strong>Unanimous Legal Heir Consent:</strong> All legal heirs holding a share in the property must sign the partition agreement.</li>
        <li><strong>Detailed Schedule of Property:</strong> Clear demarcation of physical boundaries, survey numbers, plot sizes, and exact door/khata numbers allocated to each party.</li>
        <li><strong>Blood Relation Stamp Duty Concessions:</strong> Most Indian states (Tamil Nadu, Karnataka, Maharashtra, UP, Telangana) offer significantly reduced stamp duty rates (ranging from 1% to 3% or fixed charges of Rs. 1,000 to Rs. 25,000) when property is partitioned strictly among immediate family members/blood relations.</li>
        <li><strong>Mandatory Registration:</strong> Under Section 17 of the Indian Registration Act 1908, a partition deed involving immovable property worth more than Rs. 100 must be registered at the jurisdiction Sub-Registrar Office (SRO). Unregistered partition deeds are inadmissible as primary title evidence in court under Section 49.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Difference Between Partition Deed and Family Settlement Agreement</h3>
    <p>While a <em>Partition Deed</em> creates instant legal division and transfers title rights at the SRO, a <em>Family Settlement Agreement (FSA)</em> is often executed orally or via informal memorandum to resolve pending family disputes. However, to effectuate mutation in revenue records (Patta, RTC, Khata), the FSA must subsequently be registered as a formal Partition Deed.</p>

    <h3>Step-by-Step Procedure for Partition Registration & Revenue Mutation</h3>
    <ol style="padding-left: 20px;">
        <li><strong>Drafting by Property Advocate:</strong> Prepare the deed specifying co-owner shares, title history, ancestral genealogy tree, and property schedules.</li>
        <li><strong>Stamp Duty Calculation:</strong> Calculate state-specific stamp duty based on market value or guideline value of the partitioned shares.</li>
        <li><strong>SRO Slot Booking:</strong> Book an appointment on Kaveri 2.0 (KA), TNREGINET (TN), IGRS TS, IGRS AP, or IGR MH.</li>
        <li><strong>Biometric Execution:</strong> All co-sharers present original IDs, PAN cards, original title deeds, and complete thumb impression/biometric signature verification.</li>
        <li><strong>Revenue Mutation:</strong> Submit the registered Partition Deed to the local Tahsildar/Municipality to issue separate Khata, Patta, or 7/12 extracts for each owner.</li>
    </ol>
